IMPORT prelude

§ `Codex Insurance Limited hospitalization policy`

-- ===========================================================================
-- A faithful, record-threaded encoding of the policy between CODEX INSURANCE
-- LIMITED ("us") and You.
--
-- SCOPE. Everything in the instrument that bears on whether a benefit is
-- payable is encoded: 1.1, 1.2, 1.3, 2.1 in full, and the operative parts of
-- 3.1, 3.2, 3.6. The clauses that are NOT operative on that question are
-- named, with a note saying why, in the section `Clauses not operative on
-- whether a benefit is payable` at the foot of this file (3.3 governing law,
-- 3.4 currency, and the manner-of-payment half of 3.5).
--
-- SHAPE. One `Claim` record carries every fact the policy makes operative. It
-- is threaded as a single `GIVEN` parameter through one named decision per
-- clause, so the call graph mirrors the section numbering:
--
--     `a benefit is payable on this claim`                       <- top level
--       |- `1.1 the claim is premised on a hospitalization for sickness or
--       |   accidental injury`
--       |- `1.1 the policy is in effect at the time of the hospitalization`
--       |    |- `3.6 the policy term had begun by the time of the hospitalization`
--       |    |- 1.1(1) signed, 1.1(2) premium paid
--       |    |- 1.1(3) -> `1.3 still pending` OR `1.3 satisfied in a timely fashion`
--       |    `- 1.1(4) -> NOT `1.2 the policy has been canceled ...`
--       |                    |- 1.2 fraud / misrepresentation / material withholding
--       |                    |- 1.2 failure of the 1.3 condition
--       |                    `- 1.2 automatic cancelation at the end of the term (3.6)
--       |- NOT `2.1 a general exclusion applies`   (2.1(1)..2.1(5))
--       |- `3.1.1 the policy insures you 24 hours a day anywhere in the world`
--       `- `3.2 Section 3.2.1 poses no bar to liability on this claim`
--
-- TIME. Every time in a claim is expressed as a NUMBER of months measured from
-- the effective date of the policy (3.6: "the date accepted by Us as signified
-- by our signature of the policy"). So the effective date is month 0, the
-- 6th month anniversary is 6, the 7th month anniversary is 7, and the end of
-- the one-year term is 12. No calendar arithmetic is needed anywhere. The one
-- period the instrument states in days rather than months -- the sixty (60)
-- days in 3.2.1 -- is carried as 2 months; see the note at that decision.
--
-- MISSING SECTION 5. Both 1.2 ("the last day of the policy term described in
-- Section 5 below") and 3.5.1 ("The premium described in Section 5 below")
-- cross-refer to a Section 5 that does not appear in the instrument. The term
-- is taken from 3.6, which does describe it. The premium AMOUNT and the
-- BENEFIT SCHEDULE are simply absent from the text, so this module answers
-- whether a benefit is payable and cannot answer how much.
-- ===========================================================================

§§ `Constants`

-- 3.6: the term "will last for a period of one year from" the effective date.
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the length of the policy term in months` MEANS 12

-- 1.3: "occurring no later than the 6th month anniversary of the effective
-- date of this policy".
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the wellness visit deadline in months` MEANS 6

-- 1.3: "No later than the 7th month anniversary of the effective date of this
-- policy, you will supply us with written confirmation".
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the written confirmation deadline in months` MEANS 7

-- 3.2.1: "such arbitration to be commenced within three (3) months from the
-- day such parties are unable to settle the dispute or difference".
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the arbitration commencement window in months` MEANS 3

-- 3.2.1: "the expiration of sixty (60) days after written proof of claim has
-- been submitted". Rendered in the months unit this module works in. See the
-- ambiguity note at `3.2.1 the sixty-day period ... has expired`.
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the sixty day period after written proof of claim, in months` MEANS 2

-- 2.1(5): "If your age at the time of the hospitalization is equal to or
-- greater than 80 years of age."
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the excluded age in years` MEANS 80

§§ `The fact schema`

-- 1.1: a benefit is premised on a hospitalization "for sickness or accidental
-- injury". An enumeration, not a string: the instrument recognises exactly two
-- qualifying grounds, and everything else falls outside the chapeau.
DECLARE `Basis of the hospitalization` IS ONE OF
    sickness
    `accidental injury`
    `neither sickness nor accidental injury`

-- 2.1: the four named origins are separate booleans rather than one enum
-- because the chapeau reads "arising directly or indirectly out of", and an
-- event can arise indirectly out of more than one of them at once (a
-- fire fighter who skydives on duty).
DECLARE Hospitalization HAS
    `months from the effective date to the hospitalization`                             IS A NUMBER
    `your age in years at the time of the hospitalization`                              IS A NUMBER
    `the basis of the hospitalization`                                                  IS A `Basis of the hospitalization`
    `the event causing it arose directly or indirectly out of skydiving`                IS A BOOLEAN
    `the event causing it arose directly or indirectly out of service in the military`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `the event causing it arose directly or indirectly out of service as a fire fighter` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the event causing it arose directly or indirectly out of service in the police`    IS A BOOLEAN

-- 1.2 names three species of disclosure default. They are NOT treated alike:
-- "fraud" stands alone, before the comma, unqualified; "misrepresentation" and
-- "material withholding" are both qualified by "of any information provided by
-- you to the Company in connection with any communication or information
-- relating to this policy"; and the adjective "material" sits only on
-- "withholding". See the ambiguity register.
DECLARE `Kind of disclosure default` IS ONE OF
    fraud
    misrepresentation
    `withholding of information`

DECLARE `Disclosure default` HAS
    `the kind of default`                                                        IS A `Kind of disclosure default`
    `the information was provided by you to the Company`                         IS A BOOLEAN
    `in connection with a communication or information relating to this policy`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `the withholding was material`                                               IS A BOOLEAN
    `months from the effective date to when it occurred`                         IS A NUMBER

-- 1.3: "a wellness visit for yourself with a qualified medical provider
-- occurring no later than the 6th month anniversary".
DECLARE `Wellness visit` HAS
    `months from the effective date to the visit`  IS A NUMBER
    `the visit was for yourself`                   IS A BOOLEAN
    `the medical provider was qualified`           IS A BOOLEAN

-- 1.3: "you will supply us with written confirmation from the medical provider
-- in question".
DECLARE `Written confirmation` HAS
    `months from the effective date to when it was supplied to us`  IS A NUMBER
    `it is in writing`                                              IS A BOOLEAN
    `it is from the medical provider in question`                   IS A BOOLEAN

-- 3.2.1. NOTHING means no dispute or disagreement has arisen, in which case
-- neither the arbitration time bar nor the award condition precedent bites.
DECLARE Dispute HAS
    `months from the effective date to the day the parties were unable to settle it`  IS A NUMBER
    `months from the effective date to the commencement of arbitration`               IS A MAYBE NUMBER
    `a valid arbitration award has been issued`                                       IS A BOOLEAN

-- THE ONE RECORD. Everything a caller must supply.
--
-- Note what is NOT here, deliberately: the place of the event and the time of
-- day (3.1.1 makes both inoperative), the currency of payment (3.4.1), and the
-- governing law (3.3.1). A field for any of those would be a fact the
-- instrument does not make operative.
DECLARE Claim HAS
    -- 1.1(1)
    `this agreement had been signed by the time of the hospitalization`                       IS A BOOLEAN
    -- 1.1(2)
    `the applicable premium for the policy period had been paid by the time of the hospitalization` IS A BOOLEAN
    -- 1.3
    `the wellness visit`                                                                      IS A MAYBE `Wellness visit`
    `the written confirmation`                                                                IS A MAYBE `Written confirmation`
    -- 1.2, first and second limbs
    `disclosure defaults`                                                                     IS A LIST OF `Disclosure default`
    -- 1.1 chapeau, 2.1, 3.6
    `the hospitalization on which the claim is premised`                                      IS A Hospitalization
    -- 3.2.1
    `the dispute or disagreement, if any`                                                     IS A MAYBE Dispute
    `months from the effective date to the submission of written proof of claim`              IS A MAYBE NUMBER
    `months from the effective date to when you seek to recover`                              IS A NUMBER

-- A projection used by almost every clause below: 1.1 fixes the moment at
-- which the policy must be in effect as "the time of the hospitalization".
GIVEN claim IS A Claim
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the month of the hospitalization` claim MEANS
    claim's `the hospitalization on which the claim is premised`'s `months from the effective date to the hospitalization`

§§ `Section 1.3 — the wellness visit condition`

-- 1.3 imposes two deadlines and three qualitative requirements:
--   the visit must be a wellness visit FOR YOURSELF, with a QUALIFIED medical
--   provider, occurring no later than month 6; and the confirmation must be
--   WRITTEN, FROM THE MEDICAL PROVIDER IN QUESTION, and supplied to us no
--   later than month 7.
-- "No later than" is an upper bound only, so both tests are AT MOST.
GIVEN claim IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `1.3 the condition has been satisfied in a timely fashion` claim IF
    CONSIDER claim's `the wellness visit`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST visit THEN
        CONSIDER claim's `the written confirmation`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
        WHEN JUST confirmation THEN
                visit's `months from the effective date to the visit` AT MOST `the wellness visit deadline in months`
            AND visit's `the visit was for yourself`
            AND visit's `the medical provider was qualified`
            AND confirmation's `months from the effective date to when it was supplied to us` AT MOST `the written confirmation deadline in months`
            AND confirmation's `it is in writing`
            AND confirmation's `it is from the medical provider in question`

-- 1.1(3) contemplates a third state alongside satisfied and unsatisfied: the
-- condition may be "still pending". A condition with a future deadline is
-- pending until that deadline passes, so pendency is keyed on month 7 and not
-- on whether timely satisfaction remains achievable. See the ambiguity
-- register: this is the reading that gives 1.1(3)'s first limb any work to do.
GIVEN claim IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `1.3 the condition is still pending at the time of the hospitalization` claim IF
        `the month of the hospitalization` claim AT MOST `the written confirmation deadline in months`
    AND NOT `1.3 the condition has been satisfied in a timely fashion` claim

§§ `Section 1.2 — cancelation`

-- The three species of 1.2 default read differently. "there is fraud" is
-- unqualified. "any misrepresentation" carries the "provided by you to the
-- Company in connection with ..." qualifier but NOT the word "material".
-- "material withholding" carries both.
GIVEN `the default` IS A `Disclosure default`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `1.2 this default triggers cancelation` `the default` IF
    CONSIDER `the default`'s `the kind of default`
    WHEN fraud THEN TRUE
    WHEN misrepresentation THEN
            `the default`'s `the information was provided by you to the Company`
        AND `the default`'s `in connection with a communication or information relating to this policy`
    WHEN `withholding of information` THEN
            `the default`'s `the withholding was material`
        AND `the default`'s `the information was provided by you to the Company`
        AND `the default`'s `in connection with a communication or information relating to this policy`

-- 1.1(4) asks whether the policy "has not been canceled" AT THE TIME OF THE
-- HOSPITALIZATION. So a cancelation trigger is only capable of defeating this
-- claim if it had already occurred by then. A misrepresentation made after the
-- hospitalization cancels the policy going forward but does not unmake a
-- benefit already earned. See the ambiguity register -- this is the single
-- most consequential reading in the file.
GIVEN claim IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `1.2 canceled for fraud, misrepresentation or material withholding` claim IF
    any (GIVEN `the default` YIELD
                `1.2 this default triggers cancelation` `the default`
            AND `the default`'s `months from the effective date to when it occurred`
                    AT MOST `the month of the hospitalization` claim)
        (claim's `disclosure defaults`)

-- 1.2's third limb: cancelation "if the condition set out in Section 1.3 has
-- not been satisfied in a timely fashion". That fact cannot be established
-- before the month 7 deadline passes, so the deemed cancelation takes effect
-- at month 7 and bites only on a hospitalization after it.
GIVEN claim IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `1.2 canceled for failure to satisfy the Section 1.3 condition in a timely fashion` claim IF
        `the month of the hospitalization` claim GREATER THAN `the written confirmation deadline in months`
    AND NOT `1.3 the condition has been satisfied in a timely fashion` claim

-- 1.2's final sentence: "It will also be automatically canceled at midnight,
-- US Eastern time then in effect, on the last day of the policy term described
-- in Section 5 below." Section 5 is absent; 3.6 describes the term as one year
-- from the effective date. Cancelation strikes at the END of the last day, so
-- month 12 itself is still inside the term and only a later hospitalization is
-- caught. See the ambiguity register.
GIVEN claim IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `1.2 automatically canceled at the end of the policy term` claim IF
    `the month of the hospitalization` claim GREATER THAN `the length of the policy term in months`

GIVEN claim IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `1.2 the policy has been canceled by the time of the hospitalization` claim IF
       `1.2 canceled for fraud, misrepresentation or material withholding` claim
    OR `1.2 canceled for failure to satisfy the Section 1.3 condition in a timely fashion` claim
    OR `1.2 automatically canceled at the end of the policy term` claim

§§ `Section 3.6 — the policy term`

-- "The term of this policy will begin on the date accepted by Us as signified
-- by our signature of the policy (the effective date)". Nothing is in effect
-- before month 0. The upper end of the term is enforced through 1.2's
-- automatic-cancelation limb above, exactly as the instrument routes it.
GIVEN claim IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `3.6 the policy term had begun by the time of the hospitalization` claim IF
    `the month of the hospitalization` claim AT LEAST 0

§§ `Section 1.1 — policy in effect, and the premise of the claim`

-- The chapeau of 1.1. "The payment of any benefit under this policy is
-- conditioned on the policy being in effect at the time of the hospitalization
-- FOR SICKNESS OR ACCIDENTAL INJURY on which the claim for such benefit is
-- premised." A hospitalization on any other basis is not a hospitalization the
-- instrument will pay a benefit on.
GIVEN claim IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `1.1 the claim is premised on a hospitalization for sickness or accidental injury` claim IF
    CONSIDER claim's `the hospitalization on which the claim is premised`'s `the basis of the hospitalization`
    WHEN sickness                                   THEN TRUE
    WHEN `accidental injury`                        THEN TRUE
    WHEN `neither sickness nor accidental injury`   THEN FALSE

-- 1.1's four numbered conditions, read as necessary and sufficient. Note that
-- 1.1(3) is a DISJUNCTION -- pending OR timely satisfied -- which is what lets
-- a hospitalization in month 3, before any wellness visit has happened, be
-- covered.
GIVEN claim IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `1.1 the policy is in effect at the time of the hospitalization` claim IF
        `3.6 the policy term had begun by the time of the hospitalization` claim
    AND claim's `this agreement had been signed by the time of the hospitalization`
    AND claim's `the applicable premium for the policy period had been paid by the time of the hospitalization`
    AND (   `1.3 the condition is still pending at the time of the hospitalization` claim
         OR `1.3 the condition has been satisfied in a timely fashion` claim )
    AND NOT `1.2 the policy has been canceled by the time of the hospitalization` claim

§§ `Section 2.1 — general exclusions`

GIVEN claim IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `2.1(1) the event arose directly or indirectly out of skydiving` claim IF
    claim's `the hospitalization on which the claim is premised`'s `the event causing it arose directly or indirectly out of skydiving`

GIVEN claim IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `2.1(2) the event arose directly or indirectly out of service in the military` claim IF
    claim's `the hospitalization on which the claim is premised`'s `the event causing it arose directly or indirectly out of service in the military`

GIVEN claim IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `2.1(3) the event arose directly or indirectly out of service as a fire fighter` claim IF
    claim's `the hospitalization on which the claim is premised`'s `the event causing it arose directly or indirectly out of service as a fire fighter`

GIVEN claim IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `2.1(4) the event arose directly or indirectly out of service in the police` claim IF
    claim's `the hospitalization on which the claim is premised`'s `the event causing it arose directly or indirectly out of service in the police`

-- 2.1(5) is grammatically detached from the chapeau: it does not name
-- something an event can arise out of, it states a condition ("If your age at
-- the time of the hospitalization is equal to or greater than 80 years of
-- age"). Encoded as a free-standing exclusion, which is what it reads as.
-- "equal to or greater than" is AT LEAST.
GIVEN claim IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `2.1(5) your age at the time of the hospitalization is 80 or greater` claim IF
    claim's `the hospitalization on which the claim is premised`'s `your age in years at the time of the hospitalization`
        AT LEAST `the excluded age in years`

-- "Your policy will not apply to, and no benefit will be paid with respect to,
-- any event causing sickness or accidental injury arising directly or
-- indirectly out of: ... or ... or ..." -- a disjunction.
GIVEN claim IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `2.1 a general exclusion applies` claim IF
       `2.1(1) the event arose directly or indirectly out of skydiving` claim
    OR `2.1(2) the event arose directly or indirectly out of service in the military` claim
    OR `2.1(3) the event arose directly or indirectly out of service as a fire fighter` claim
    OR `2.1(4) the event arose directly or indirectly out of service in the police` claim
    OR `2.1(5) your age at the time of the hospitalization is 80 or greater` claim

§§ `Section 3.1 — where the policy applies`

-- 3.1.1 "Your Policy insures You twenty-four (24) hours a day anywhere in the
-- world." This is coverage-granting and imposes no condition: it is here, and
-- wired into the top-level conjunction, so that the call graph records
-- affirmatively that NEITHER the place NOR the time of day of the event is an
-- operative fact. It is constitutively TRUE, with no fact dependency.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`3.1.1 the policy insures you 24 hours a day anywhere in the world` MEANS TRUE

§§ `Section 3.2 — arbitration`

-- "such arbitration to be commenced within three (3) months from the day such
-- parties are unable to settle the dispute or difference". Within three months
-- FROM that day: a window with both ends, [d, d + 3].
GIVEN dispute IS A Dispute
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the month the parties were unable to settle` dispute MEANS
    dispute's `months from the effective date to the day the parties were unable to settle it`

GIVEN dispute IS A Dispute
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the last month in which arbitration may be commenced` dispute MEANS
    (`the month the parties were unable to settle` dispute) PLUS `the arbitration commencement window in months`

GIVEN dispute IS A Dispute
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `3.2.1 arbitration was commenced within three months` dispute IF
    CONSIDER dispute's `months from the effective date to the commencement of arbitration`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST `the month of commencement` THEN
            `the month of commencement` AT LEAST (`the month the parties were unable to settle` dispute)
        AND `the month of commencement` AT MOST (`the last month in which arbitration may be commenced` dispute)

-- "If You fail to commence arbitration in accordance with this clause, it is
-- agreed that any cause of action and any right to make a claim that You have
-- or may have against Us shall be extinguished completely."
GIVEN dispute IS A Dispute
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `3.2.1 your right to make a claim has been extinguished` dispute IF
    NOT `3.2.1 arbitration was commenced within three months` dispute

-- "Where there is a dispute or disagreement, the issuance of a valid
-- arbitration award shall also be a condition precedent to our liability under
-- this Policy."
GIVEN dispute IS A Dispute
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `3.2.1 the condition precedent of a valid arbitration award is satisfied` dispute IF
    dispute's `a valid arbitration award has been issued`

-- Both limbs are conditioned on there being a dispute or disagreement at all.
-- Where none has arisen, 3.2.1 says nothing about this claim.
GIVEN claim IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `3.2 Section 3.2.1 poses no bar to liability on this claim` claim IF
    CONSIDER claim's `the dispute or disagreement, if any`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN TRUE
    WHEN JUST dispute THEN
            NOT `3.2.1 your right to make a claim has been extinguished` dispute
        AND `3.2.1 the condition precedent of a valid arbitration award is satisfied` dispute

§§ `The top-level decision`

-- IS A BENEFIT PAYABLE ON THIS CLAIM?
--
-- A caller supplies one `Claim` and nothing else.
--
-- 1.1 conditions payment on the policy being in effect at the time of a
-- hospitalization for sickness or accidental injury; 2.1 says that where an
-- exclusion applies "no benefit will be paid"; 3.2.1 extinguishes the right to
-- claim, and makes an arbitration award a condition precedent to OUR
-- LIABILITY, where a dispute has arisen. Each of those speaks directly to
-- whether a benefit is payable, so each is a conjunct here.
--
-- 3.2.1's sixty-day rule is NOT a conjunct: it governs when You may SEEK TO
-- RECOVER, not whether a benefit is payable. It is applied in the separate
-- decision below.
GIVEN claim IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a benefit is payable on this claim` claim IF
        `1.1 the claim is premised on a hospitalization for sickness or accidental injury` claim
    AND `1.1 the policy is in effect at the time of the hospitalization` claim
    AND NOT `2.1 a general exclusion applies` claim
    AND `3.1.1 the policy insures you 24 hours a day anywhere in the world`
    AND `3.2 Section 3.2.1 poses no bar to liability on this claim` claim

§§ `A downstream question: may You sue on it yet?`

-- "In no case shall You seek to recover on this Policy before the expiration
-- of sixty (60) days after written proof of claim has been submitted to Us in
-- accordance with the provisions of this Policy."
--
-- Where no written proof of claim has been submitted at all, the sixty days
-- have not begun to run and the bar stands.
--
-- UNIT NOTE: sixty days is carried as 2 months, the nearest expression of it
-- in the unit this module works in. Sixty days is shorter than two calendar
-- months in every month-pair except February-plus-a-short-month, so this is
-- very slightly conservative against You. See the ambiguity register.
GIVEN claim IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `3.2.1 the sixty day period after written proof of claim has expired` claim IF
    CONSIDER claim's `months from the effective date to the submission of written proof of claim`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST `the month of submission` THEN
        claim's `months from the effective date to when you seek to recover`
            AT LEAST (`the month of submission` PLUS `the sixty day period after written proof of claim, in months`)

GIVEN claim IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `you may seek to recover on this policy now` claim IF
        `a benefit is payable on this claim` claim
    AND `3.2.1 the sixty day period after written proof of claim has expired` claim

§§ `Clauses not operative on whether a benefit is payable`

-- 3.3.1 "Your Policy is governed by the laws of New York." A choice-of-law
-- term. It selects the body of law under which every clause above is to be
-- read; it is not itself a fact about a claim, and no value of it makes a
-- benefit payable or unpayable. NOT ENCODED, deliberately.
--
-- 3.4.1 "All payments by You to Us and by Us to You or someone else under your
-- policy must be in United States currency." A term about the DENOMINATION of
-- a payment, once a payment is due. It bears on how a benefit is paid, not on
-- whether one is payable. NOT ENCODED as an operative fact. (It would matter
-- to a module that computed the benefit amount -- which this one cannot,
-- because the schedule lives in the missing Section 5.)
--
-- 3.5.1 "The premium described in Section 5 below shall be paid in one lump
-- sum at the signing of the policy." The MANNER and TIMING half of this is not
-- encoded; what is encoded is the fact 1.1(2) makes operative, namely that
-- "the applicable premium for the policy period has been paid". Whether an
-- instalment-paying insured who has paid the whole premium satisfies 1.1(2) is
-- recorded in the ambiguity register.
--
-- 3.6's first half IS encoded (`3.6 the policy term had begun ...`) and its
-- second half is enforced through 1.2's automatic-cancelation limb, which is
-- how the instrument itself routes it.

§§ `Worked example`

-- A single fixture, present only to show a caller what supplying facts looks
-- like, and to prove the module evaluates rather than merely typechecking.
-- Hospitalization in month 3 for sickness; wellness visit not yet made, so the
-- 1.3 condition is still pending and 1.1(3) is met on its first limb.

`an ordinary claim` MEANS Claim WITH
    `this agreement had been signed by the time of the hospitalization` IS TRUE
    `the applicable premium for the policy period had been paid by the time of the hospitalization` IS TRUE
    `the wellness visit` IS NOTHING
    `the written confirmation` IS NOTHING
    `disclosure defaults` IS EMPTY
    `the hospitalization on which the claim is premised` IS Hospitalization WITH
        `months from the effective date to the hospitalization` IS 3
        `your age in years at the time of the hospitalization` IS 41
        `the basis of the hospitalization` IS sickness
        `the event causing it arose directly or indirectly out of skydiving` IS FALSE
        `the event causing it arose directly or indirectly out of service in the military` IS FALSE
        `the event causing it arose directly or indirectly out of service as a fire fighter` IS FALSE
        `the event causing it arose directly or indirectly out of service in the police` IS FALSE
    `the dispute or disagreement, if any` IS NOTHING
    `months from the effective date to the submission of written proof of claim` IS JUST 4
    `months from the effective date to when you seek to recover` IS 7

#EVAL `a benefit is payable on this claim` `an ordinary claim`

#EVAL `you may seek to recover on this policy now` `an ordinary claim`

§§ `Ambiguity register`

-- Every place the text admitted more than one reading, the reading taken, and
-- why. An ambiguity resolved silently would be a defect in this encoding; each
-- one below is a finding about the instrument.
--
-- A1. 1.2 / 1.1(4) -- WHEN a deemed cancelation takes effect.
--     Readings: (a) cancelation is deemed to occur when its trigger occurs, so
--     1.1(4) is failed only by a trigger predating the hospitalization;
--     (b) "will be deemed to have occurred" is retroactive to inception, so a
--     trigger at ANY time defeats every claim under the policy.
--     TAKEN: (a). 1.1 fixes the moment of assessment as "at the time of the
--     hospitalization", and 1.1(3) expressly contemplates a condition that is
--     "still pending" at that moment -- a clause with no work to do under (b).
--     This is the single most consequential choice in the file: under (b) a
--     misrepresentation discovered during claims handling would unmake an
--     otherwise good claim. See `1.2 canceled for fraud, misrepresentation or
--     material withholding`, which carries the AT MOST test.
--
-- A2. 1.1(3) -- what "still pending" means.
--     Readings: (a) the month-7 deadline has not passed and the condition is
--     not yet satisfied; (b) timely satisfaction is still ACHIEVABLE, so at
--     month 6.5 with no wellness visit yet the condition has already failed
--     rather than being pending.
--     TAKEN: (a). "Pending" is the ordinary word for an obligation whose
--     deadline has not arrived, and 1.2 keys cancelation on the condition
--     having "not been satisfied in a timely fashion" -- a fact that cannot be
--     established before the deadline passes. Consequence: a hospitalization
--     in month 6 with no visit at all is covered.
--
-- A3. 1.2 -- the scope of "material".
--     Readings: (a) "any misrepresentation" OR "material withholding" -- the
--     adjective sits only on withholding, so an immaterial misrepresentation
--     cancels; (b) materiality is implied for both limbs.
--     TAKEN: (a), the grammar of the sentence. Recorded because it is harsh
--     and probably not intended: it makes any inaccuracy in any communication
--     "relating to this policy" a cancelation trigger.
--
-- A4. 1.2 -- whether "fraud" carries the "provided by you to the Company"
--     qualifier.
--     Readings: (a) fraud stands alone before the comma and is unqualified, so
--     fraud by anyone in any connection triggers cancelation; (b) the whole
--     trailing qualifier distributes over all three species.
--     TAKEN: (a), the grammar. Consequence: the `fraud` arm of
--     `1.2 this default triggers cancelation` reads TRUE without looking at
--     the two qualifier fields.
--
-- A5. 1.2 / 3.6 -- the last instant of the policy term.
--     Readings: (a) inclusive -- cancelation strikes at midnight ENDING the
--     anniversary day, so month 12 is still inside the term; (b) exclusive --
--     a period of one year from the effective date closes at the start of the
--     anniversary, so month 12 is out.
--     TAKEN: (a). 1.2 says the cancelation is "at midnight ... on the last day
--     of the policy term", i.e. at the end of that day, and 3.6 runs the term
--     "for a period of one year FROM" the effective date. Encoded as
--     GREATER THAN 12 for the automatic-cancelation limb.
--
-- A6. 1.2 -- the dangling cross-reference to "Section 5".
--     Section 5 does not exist in this instrument. 3.6 describes the policy
--     term and 3.5.1 describes the premium, and 3.5.1 ALSO cross-refers to the
--     missing Section 5.
--     TAKEN: the term is read from 3.6 (one year from the effective date).
--     The premium amount and the benefit schedule are simply absent, so this
--     module answers whether a benefit is payable, never how much.
--
-- A7. 1.3 -- whether the wellness visit has a lower time bound.
--     Readings: (a) literal -- "no later than the 6th month anniversary" is an
--     upper bound only, so a visit before the effective date would qualify;
--     (b) purposive -- the visit must fall within the policy period.
--     TAKEN: (a). The text states one bound and this encoding states one bound.
--
-- A8. 1.3 -- whether the confirmation must postdate the visit.
--     The text does not say so. Not encoded; a confirmation supplied in month
--     2 of a visit in month 5 satisfies the encoded test. Recorded rather than
--     silently repaired.
--
-- A9. 2.1(5) -- whether age is an exclusion in its own right.
--     Readings: (a) free-standing -- "If your age at the time of the
--     hospitalization is equal to or greater than 80" is a condition, not
--     something an event can arise out of, so it excludes regardless of cause;
--     (b) grammatical -- it is item 5 in a list governed by "arising directly
--     or indirectly out of", which would make it nonsense.
--     TAKEN: (a). The item's own "If ..." construction breaks the parallel of
--     items 1-4 and reads as a stand-alone proviso.
--
-- A10. 2.1 chapeau -- what "arising directly or indirectly out of" modifies.
--     Readings: (a) the EVENT arises out of the named activity; (b) the
--     SICKNESS OR INJURY arises out of it.
--     TAKEN: (a), the nearer antecedent ("any event causing sickness or
--     accidental injury arising ... out of"). The field names say "the event
--     causing it arose ...". Immaterial on any fact pattern I can construct,
--     but recorded.
--
-- A11. 2.1(2)-(4) -- whose service.
--     "Service in the military", "as a fire fighter", "in the police" name no
--     person. TAKEN: Your service, since the clause opens "Your policy will
--     not apply to". An injury arising out of a THIRD party's police service
--     (being struck by a patrol car) is therefore not excluded on this
--     reading -- an outcome worth flagging.
--
-- A12. 1.1 -- whether the four numbered conditions are exhaustive.
--     Readings: (a) "The policy will be in effect IF" is definitional, i.e.
--     if and only if; (b) the list is sufficient but not necessary.
--     TAKEN: (a), the drafting convention for a definitional list. Under (b)
--     the clause could not do the work 1.1's chapeau assigns it.
--
-- A13. 1.1(2) with 3.5.1 -- what counts as the premium having been paid.
--     3.5.1 requires the premium to be "paid in one lump sum at the signing".
--     Readings: (a) 1.1(2) asks only whether the premium for the period has in
--     fact been paid, and 3.5.1 is a separate promise about manner and timing;
--     (b) a premium paid in instalments is not "the applicable premium ...
--     paid" for 1.1(2) purposes.
--     TAKEN: (a). Only the fact of payment is a field; the manner is not.
--
-- A14. 3.2.1 -- what counts as "a dispute or disagreement".
--     If a declined claim is itself a disagreement, then a valid arbitration
--     award is a condition precedent to liability on essentially every
--     contested claim, and the whole policy is arbitrate-first. The text gives
--     no test. TAKEN: modelled as a supplied fact -- `the dispute or
--     disagreement, if any` -- so the caller, not this encoding, decides.
--     Where it is NOTHING, 3.2.1 says nothing about the claim.
--
-- A15. 3.2.1 -- "the day such parties are unable to settle".
--     Vague: no test is given for when the parties become unable to settle.
--     TAKEN: supplied as a fact rather than derived.
--
-- A16. 3.2.1 -- sixty days in a months-denominated model.
--     Sixty days is not two months. TAKEN: 2 months, the nearest expression in
--     this module's unit, bound once as
--     `the sixty day period after written proof of claim, in months` so the
--     approximation sits in one place. Slightly conservative against You.
--
-- A17. 3.2.1's sixty-day rule -- whether it bears on payability.
--     Readings: (a) it governs when You may SEEK TO RECOVER, a maturity rule,
--     not whether a benefit is payable; (b) it is a bar on the claim itself.
--     TAKEN: (a). The top-level decision omits it; the separate decision
--     `you may seek to recover on this policy now` conjoins it, so a caller
--     who wants reading (b) has it one call away.
--
-- A18. 1.1 chapeau -- a claim not premised on a hospitalization at all.
--     The instrument only ever speaks of benefits premised on "the
--     hospitalization for sickness or accidental injury". TAKEN: the Claim
--     record always carries a Hospitalization, and a hospitalization on some
--     other footing is represented by the third arm of
--     `Basis of the hospitalization`. A purported claim with no
--     hospitalization behind it is outside this model's domain.
--
-- A19. 1.1(1) -- "This agreement is signed", by whom.
--     3.6 defines the effective date by OUR signature, so our signature exists
--     by construction of the time axis; 1.1(1) must therefore be doing work
--     about Your signature (or about both). TAKEN: one boolean covering
--     whether the agreement stands signed at the time of the hospitalization.
